theory.intro:
A **cylinder** has the same circular cross-section all the way through, so $V = Ah$ still applies. The area of a circle is $A = \pi r^{2}$, which gives $V = \pi r^{2}h$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.7]
\draw (-2.4,0)--(-2.4,5);
\draw (2.4,0)--(2.4,5) node[midway,right] {$h$};
\draw (0,5) ellipse (2.4 and 0.72);
\draw (-2.4,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=360,x radius=2.4,y radius=0.72];
\draw[dashed] (-2.4,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=0,x radius=2.4,y radius=0.72];
\fill (0,5) circle (2pt);
\draw (0,5)--(2.4,5) node[midway,above,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$r$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

